  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1103 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nThere are a few ways to continue saying that the synoptic pattern \naloft remains unchanged with a large and blocked high pressure \nsystem situated over the south-central CONUS. Despite this a lower-\nlevel low pressure system is still present in northeast Colorado and \nenabling convection along the western Great Plains. This is the only \nconvection, and pressingly notable weather, for the near future for \nthe CWA. Outside of normally seasonal heat, impactful weather is not \nforecast currently.\n\nLooking at tonights convection, it comes in two parts. The first is \ncurrently ongoing in extreme SW Kansas and into the panhandles. \nOutside of in the Oklahoma panhandle, no strong winds (>45 mph) have \nbeen reported It is expected to stay that way unless a stronger cell \nis able to initiate, or a portion of organized convection can \ndevelop a cold pool. At least for the time being, neither of these \nthings are expected to develop. With only modest CAPE and shear (750 \nJ/kg and 30 KTs), the severe threat is limited with most of the \nstorms trending downward. The second mode of storms tonight comes \nwith an MCS and associated cold front coming out of Nebraska. Model \nruns in the previous days suggested that the complex could clip \nHays, but the last 24 hours, CAM runs have trended away from that \ngreatly. However, some CAMs have varying amounts of convection \ninitiating along the southwestern flank that may extend into the \nforecast area near Hays. This will warrant monitoring for severe \npotential, however runs into the same problem as the rest of the \nconvection with not the most supportive environment. Once the storms \ndissolve and the cold front passes through later tonight, the \npattern becomes benign. \n\nThis weekend's highs behind the cold front is forecast to be in the \n90s. Into next week, highs will tick up back towards the 100 degree \nmark. The apparent temperatures on Thursday/Friday rise up towards \nthe 102-105 degree line, and may require a Heat Advisory. This is \nespecially true as the long-ranged ensembles have been a few degrees \ntoo cool with this pattern. If the previous few weeks continue in \nthis manner. Apparent temperatures as hot as 110 degrees are \npossible in the southeastern zones near the Oklahoma border. The \nsame long ranged ensembles tease a low probability of precipitation \non Wednesday night strictly near the Colorado border. With the low \nchances (<20%) and temporal uncertainty, it is just something to be \naware of at this stage.",
